am Gate and the screen wall along the street; behind it the Portland-stone south wing and quadrangle of the palace, balustraded; the Royal Mews to the south round its yard with the clock-tower gate. From general knowledge; the OpenStreetMap ground gave the palace mass and the street.  The gallery's footprint, the column count and the Mews' layout are approximate. Refined 6 Oct 2026 from published descriptions and the ground's OpenStreetMap outlines, not photographs. The museum's point lies about 140 m south of the palace's middle, at the south wing, and the outlines (palace storeys of seven, the culverted Tyburn drawn as water running under it) put the palace's main block further east than first drawn: the palace moved 30 m east, the King's Gallery 24 m west and 6 m north onto the palace's south-east tip where the outlines end, Buckingham Gate with it, and the Royal Mews 20 m west toward its outline beside Buckingham Palace Road. The palace and the gallery now read as pale Portland stone (they were drawn in a darker stone), and the palace has its rows of windows on every side, opened so they read one by one. Still guessed: the palace's quadrangle size (the outline fills it), the gallery's footprint, the column count and the Mews' layout."}